@@ -0,0 +1,178 @@
+## Deploying with Kamal (one box)
+
+Fizzy ships with a one-box [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org/) config: a single
+Linux server with Docker, SQLite, everything in one container, SSL terminated
+by `kamal-proxy`. Background jobs run on the box inside Puma via Solid Queue.
+
+Fork the repo, fill in your hostname, your mail server, and your secrets, run
+`bin/kamal setup`, and you get Fizzy at `https://your-host` with data that
+survives the next deploy.
+
+### What you get
+
+- `config/deploy.yml`: single `web` host, `proxy.ssl: true` for automatic
+  Let's Encrypt certificates, local registry (`localhost:5555`) so no Docker
+  Hub account is needed, `builder.arch: amd64`.
+- `.kamal/secrets`: dotenv file that reads secrets from your local ENV
+  (`SECRET_KEY_BASE`, `SMTP_PASSWORD`). Safe for git, no raw credentials.
+- `volumes: ["fizzy_storage:/rails/storage"]`: SQLite databases
+  (`storage/production*.sqlite3`) and Active Storage files
+  (`storage/production/files`) persist across deploys.
+- `asset_path: /rails/public/assets`: shares fingerprinted assets between
+  versions.
+- `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: true`: web + jobs in one container. See
+  `config/puma.rb` (enabled by default for OSS) and `config/queue.yml`.
+- `config/environments/production.rb`: reads `APP_HOST` for
+  `default_url_options` and `SMTP_*` for `smtp_settings`. `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`
+  is read by `ApplicationMailer`. All are injected by Kamal `env`.
+
+### Prerequisites
+
+- A Linux server (e.g. Ubuntu 24.04) with SSH access as `root` (or set
+  `ssh.user` in `config/deploy.yml`). Kamal bootstraps Docker on first setup.
+- A public DNS `A` record pointing your hostname at the server. Let's Encrypt
+  needs ports 80 and 443 open and a single server.
+- SMTP credentials for magic-link and notification emails (any provider or
+  your own Postfix). You need at least `SMTP_ADDRESS`; most providers also
+  need port, username, and password.
+- Docker running locally (for building) and this repo's bundle installed.
+
+### 1. Fill in your hostname and mail server
+
+Edit `config/deploy.yml` (every spot is marked `TODO`):
+
+```yaml
+servers:
+  web:
+    - 203.0.113.10 # your server IP or hostname
+
+proxy:
+  ssl: true
+  host: fizzy.example.com # your public hostname
+
+env:
+  clear:
+    APP_HOST: fizzy.example.com # same as proxy host
+    MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS: "Fizzy <fizzy@fizzy.example.com>"
+    SMTP_ADDRESS: smtp.example.com
+    SMTP_PORT: 587
+    SMTP_DOMAIN: fizzy.example.com # usually same as APP_HOST
+    SMTP_USERNAME: fizzy@example.com # remove if no auth
+```
+
+`APP_HOST` must match `proxy.host`. It is used for links in emails and jobs.
+`SMTP_*` maps directly to `action_mailer.smtp_settings` in
+`config/environments/production.rb`:
+
+| Kamal `env.clear`            | `smtp_settings`        | Default                          |
+| ---------------------------- | ---------------------- | -------------------------------- |
+| `SMTP_ADDRESS`               | `address`              | (required)                       |
+| `SMTP_PORT`                  | `port`                 | `587`                            |
+| `SMTP_DOMAIN`                | `domain`               | `APP_HOST` or `localhost`        |
+| `SMTP_USERNAME`/`SMTP_USER_NAME` | `user_name`        | none (no auth)                   |
+| `SMTP_PASSWORD` (secret)     | `password`             | none                             |
+| `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION`        | `authentication`       | `plain` if username, else none   |
+| `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO`  | `enable_starttls_auto` | `true`                           |
+| `SMTP_TLS`                   | `tls`                  | only set when `true` (port 465)  |
+
+Delete `SMTP_USERNAME`/`SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` if your relay needs no auth.
+For implicit TLS (usually port 465) set `SMTP_TLS: true` and
+`SMTP_PORT: 465`.
+
+If your server is ARM (Ampere, Graviton), set `builder.arch: arm64`.
+If you prefer Docker Hub / GHCR over the local registry, see
+"Using an external registry" below.
+
+### 2. Fill in your secrets
+
+`.kamal/secrets` reads from your local ENV. Generate `SECRET_KEY_BASE` once
+and export both required secrets before every `bin/kamal` command that needs
+them (`setup`, `deploy`, `console`, ...):
+
+```sh
+openssl rand -hex 64
+# or: bin/rails secret
+
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE=<generated value>
+export SMTP_PASSWORD=<your smtp password>
+```
+
+Keep `SECRET_KEY_BASE` stable: changing it invalidates sessions and signed
+URLs. Store it in a password manager.
+
+Optional web push: generate VAPID keys as in
+[Development](development.md#web-push-notifications), export
+`VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY`/`VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`, and uncomment them in both
+`.kamal/secrets` and `config/deploy.yml` (`env.secret`).
+
+### 3. Setup and deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup   # bootstraps Docker, proxy, registry, builds + runs
+open https://fizzy.example.com
+```
+
+`bin/kamal setup` is `bin/kamal` from this repo (plain Kamal using
+`config/deploy.yml`; the `fizzy-saas` gem uses its own config only when SaaS
+mode is enabled). On success the proxy serves your host over HTTPS and
+`bin/docker-entrypoint` runs `db:prepare`, creating the SQLite databases in
+the `fizzy_storage` volume on first boot.
+
+Later deploys:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy        # build + rolling redeploy, data stays in fizzy_storage
+bin/kamal logs -f       # or: bin/kamal logs
+bin/kamal console       # bin/rails console on the box
+bin/kamal shell         # bash on the box
+bin/kamal dbc           # sqlite console (bin/rails dbconsole)
+```
+
+### Data, jobs, and SSL
+
+- **Data survives redeploys.** Everything under `/rails/storage` lives in the
+  `fizzy_storage` Docker volume. Inspect it with
+  `ssh root@your-server "docker volume inspect fizzy_storage"`. Back it up
+  before major changes (stop the app or snapshot the volume, then copy the
+  `*.sqlite3` files and `production/files`).
+- **Jobs run on the box.** `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: true` starts Solid Queue
+  inside Puma (`config/puma.rb`, `config/queue.yml`,
+  `config/recurring.yml`). No `job` role is needed for one box. If you grow
+  to multiple hosts, add the commented `job` role in `config/deploy.yml` and
+  set `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: false` on web.
+- **SSL comes from the proxy.** `proxy.ssl: true` with a single host gets a
+  Let's Encrypt certificate automatically. Rails is already set for a
+  terminating proxy (`assume_ssl` + `force_ssl` default true in
+  `config/environments/production.rb`). Keep DNS pointed at the box and 443
+  open.
+
+### Using an external registry
+
+The default `registry.server: localhost:5555` needs no account: Kamal runs a
+throwaway registry locally and forwards it over SSH. For Docker Hub / GHCR /
+ECR instead:
+
+```yaml
+image: your-user/fizzy
+registry:
+  server: ghcr.io # or registry.digitalocean.com, hub.docker.com (omit server for Hub)
+  username: your-user
+  password:
+    - KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD
+```
+
+```sh
+export KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=<token>
+# and uncomment KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD in .kamal/secrets
+```
+
+### Troubleshooting
+
+- `bin/kamal config` (with `SECRET_KEY_BASE`/`SMTP_PASSWORD` exported) prints
+  the resolved config without deploying.
+- `bin/kamal proxy logs` shows Let's Encrypt / routing issues; `bin/kamal app logs`
+  shows Rails logs.
+- Health checks hit `/up` (`config/routes.rb`). A bad `SECRET_KEY_BASE` or a
+  missing volume shows up there first.
+- SMTP failures are retried/deduped by `SmtpDeliveryErrorHandling`; check
+  `bin/kamal app logs` and your provider's dashboard.
